# bgp-rs-community-strip-multi-fastpath -- the sibling of
# bgp-rs-community-strip-multi, driven through the route-server FAST-PATH rail.
#
# VALIDATES: spec-fixit-rs-community-strip-arity AC-6 -- the two rails strip
# identically. This one covers reactor/forward_rs.go:347, which carries its own
# copy of the strip emission; the general rail is covered by the sibling file.
#
# PREVENTS: a fix landing on one rail only. The rails are selected by config
# (rs-fast-path on the SOURCE peer), so a one-sided fix would leak on whichever
# path a deployment happens to select, with both files' assertions otherwise
# identical and one of them green.
#
# PREVENTS: the leak this test was written for. wireu.StripControlCommunities
# (internal/component/bgp/wireu/community.go:158) accumulates EVERY matching
# control community into ONE slice, and the rail passes that whole slice as a
# single AttrModRemove operation. The consuming helper, removeValues in
# internal/component/bgp/plugins/filter_community/handler.go, used to accept a
# buffer of EXACTLY one value and otherwise return the list untouched -- silently,
# with the comment "caller bug, silently preserve data". So a route tagged with
# two or more control communities had NONE of them removed and the route server's
# internal steering tags reached its clients. Exactly one worked, which is why
# this went unnoticed. There was no test on this path at all.
#
# WHY THE RAIL IS THE FAST PATH: rs-fast-path is a SOURCE-side gate
# (bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath-fallback.ci documents this), so setting it on the
# SOURCE peer routes this UPDATE through reactorForwardRS instead of the bgp-rs
# ForwardCached chain.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=2
option=timeout:value=15s
option=bind:value=0.0.0.0
# Map conn=N by remote IP rather than accept order, and handshake both peers
# before running any conn script -- see bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci for why the
# accept-order default makes this shape flaky (peer_connmap.go).
option=conn_map:value=remote-ip

# Source peer (conn=1, AS 65001) announces 10.0.0.0/24 tagged with FIVE
# communities, control and ordinary INTERLEAVED so the test also proves the
# survivors keep their relative order:
#
#   0000FDE6  0:64998        control -> stripped. Blacklists AS 64998, which is
#                            not a peer here, so the forwarding decision is
#                            untouched (AC-8).
#   FDE90064  65001:100      ORDINARY -> must survive. High half is 65001,
#                            neither 0 nor the route server's 65000.
#   0000FDE7  0:64999        control -> stripped. Blacklists another absent AS.
#   FDE8FDEA  65000:65002    control -> stripped. This is the RS:X form, so it
#                            also exercises the second strip rule. Semantically
#                            it WHITELISTS 65002 (community.go:128-133), which is
#                            the receiver, so the route is still forwarded --
#                            deliberate: it proves the decision keeps working
#                            while the tag itself is removed.
#   FDE900C8  65001:200      ORDINARY -> must survive, after 65001:100.
#
# Attribute: C0 08 14 (optional transitive, COMMUNITY, 20 bytes) + the five values.
# The rest is the standard shape from bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci: ORIGIN=IGP,
# AS_PATH=[65001], NEXT_HOP=1.1.1.1, LOCAL_PREF=100, NLRI 10.0.0.0/24.
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF004D02000000324001010040020602010000FDE94003040101010140050400000064C008140000FDE6FDE900640000FDE7FDE8FDEAFDE900C8180A0000

# The source peer's own End-of-RIB, sent by its initial sync at establishment.
expect=bgp:conn=1:seq=2:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF00170200000000

# Receiver peer (conn=2, AS 65002): the forwarded UPDATE must carry a COMMUNITY
# attribute holding EXACTLY the two ordinary values, in order.
#
#   D0    flags: optional transitive + extended length. genericCommunityHandler
#         always writes the extended form (handler.go:128), so the forwarded
#         attribute header differs from the source's C0 even though the strip is
#         the only change -- do not "correct" this to C0.
#   08    COMMUNITY
#   0008  eight bytes: TWO values remain of the five sent.
#   FDE90064 FDE900C8   65001:100 then 65001:200.
#
# This single positive assertion is the whole test, and it is exact enough to be
# a negative one too. Before the fix the attribute was forwarded untouched as
# D0 08 0014 followed by all five values, so this byte string was absent. It also
# cannot be satisfied by a withdraw (a withdraw carries no path attributes) nor
# by the source's own echo (these bytes only exist after the rewrite).
#
# NO End-of-RIB rule for conn=2, deliberately: bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci MEASURED
# the forward beating the receiver's EOR onto the wire (4 failures in 6 with such
# a rule present, 0 in 6 without), and an unmatched EOR is silently swallowed by
# checker.go rather than re-offered, so a second rule here would be unmatchable.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:contains=D0080008FDE90064FDE900C8
EOF_PEER

# Observer: wait until the route server has replayed to every peer, then stop.
tmpfs=shutdown-after-up.run:mode=755:terminator=EOF_RUN
#!/usr/bin/env python3
from ze_api import API

API().run_rs_observer(expected_peers=2, forward_prefix="10.0.0.0/24")
EOF_RUN

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal rs {
		use bgp-rs
	}
	external shutdown-after-up {
		run ./shutdown-after-up.run
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer source-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			# LOAD-BEARING, and its absence is why an earlier draft of this test
			# saw all five communities arrive intact even against the FIXED
			# binary: session/rs-client defaults to false, and both the strip
			# emission (reactor_api_forward.go:642 `facts.rsClient`) and the
			# RFC 7947 policy branch above it are gated on it. Without the leaf
			# no AttrModRemove is ever accumulated, so there is nothing for the
			# community handler to do and the attribute is copied through.
			rs-client true
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		# The source-side flag that selects the fast-path rail. This is the ONLY
		# difference from bgp-rs-community-strip-multi.ci; keep the two files in
		# step, since divergence anywhere else would let one rail pass on an
		# assertion the other never makes.
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
			rs-fast-path enable
		}
	}
	peer receiver-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.2
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			# LOAD-BEARING, and its absence is why an earlier draft of this test
			# saw all five communities arrive intact even against the FIXED
			# binary: session/rs-client defaults to false, and both the strip
			# emission (reactor_api_forward.go:642 `facts.rsClient`) and the
			# RFC 7947 policy branch above it are gated on it. Without the leaf
			# no AttrModRemove is ever accumulated, so there is nothing for the
			# community handler to do and the attribute is copied through.
			rs-client true
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
# The arity refusal added by spec-fixit-rs-community-strip-arity. It must NOT
# fire here: this scenario sends a whole number of four-byte values, so seeing it
# would mean the strip buffer had become malformed.
reject=stderr:pattern=remove buffer is not a whole number of wire values
